# Line endings, settled once rather than per machine.
#
# Windows runners check out with core.autocrlf=true by default, which rewrites
# every LF to CRLF in the working tree. Prettier's endOfLine is "lf", so the
# format gate then reported 210 files as badly formatted on Windows while
# passing on Linux — a red build caused entirely by the checkout, with nothing
# in the diff to fix.
#
# `eol=lf` checks text files out as LF everywhere, so both platforms see the
# bytes that are actually committed. Nothing in this project needs CRLF: the
# extension is loaded by Chrome, and the only .cmd or .ps1 files are npm's own
# inside node_modules, which is not tracked.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so git never inspects or rewrites a byte. Without this a font or a
# PNG that happens to look like text to git's heuristic can be corrupted by
# normalisation — and a corrupted .woff2 fails silently as a missing glyph.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary

# Generated, and not worth reading in a diff or counting as authored code.
package-lock.json  -diff linguist-generated
site/package-lock.json -diff linguist-generated

# Mirrored third-party pages, kept verbatim so the challenge suite does not
# depend on a site being up. They are not this project's code.
e2e/challenges/pages/** linguist-vendored
